# Next.js App Router

## Server vs Client

- **Default = Server Components** — fetch data, keep secrets/tokens server-side, smaller JS.
- **`"use client"` only** when you need: event handlers, `useState`/`useEffect`, browser APIs, client libraries (Zustand hooks).
- Push client boundaries to **leaves** — pages/layouts stay server; interactive widgets are client children.
- Pass server-fetched data as **props** into client components (e.g. Settings inbox).

## Data & mutations

- Read domain data via **ports** from Server Components / Server Actions — not ad-hoc `fetch` in JSX.
- Mutations: **Server Actions** (`"use server"`) → use-case → adapter → API → `revalidatePath` / `redirect`.
- Do not use Server Actions as a general-purpose GET replacement when parallel reads matter.
- Session lane/role gates on the server; never trust client-only role flags.

## Routing & tenancy

- Route groups: `(auth)`, `(admin)`; keep `(trainer)` / `(client)` viable.
- Staff with 0 gyms: Settings-only shell (`/admin/settings`); ops under `(ops)` require a GymOrg.
- Shared UI: `components/ui`, `lib/*` (HTTP kernel, session, theme). Module UI: `modules/<module>/components/`. Admin chrome: `components/admin/`.

## State

- Follow rule `state-management.mdc` — server owns API truth; Zustand only for shared client UI chrome.
- Theme tokens from `docs/ui-theme.md` — no raw brand hex in features.

## Libs

- Before inventing Next/React APIs, use **Context7 MCP** (rule `context7-docs.mdc`). Prefer `node_modules/next/dist/docs/` when AGENTS.md Next block applies.
